<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sarmizegetusa Regia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sarmizegetusa Regia was the capital of the Dacian kingdom, built on mountain terraces at about 1,200 metres in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orastie-mountains">Orăștie Mountains</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. It had a fortress, a sacred zone of circular and rectangular sanctuaries, and workshops. Roman forces captured and destroyed it in 106 CE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>'s second Dacian War. It is part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sarmizegetusa-regia.jpg" alt="Sarmizegetusa Regia" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cosmin Stefanescu · CC BY-SA 3.0 ro · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sarmisegetusa_Regia_-_Templele_patrulatere_mici_-_Zona_sacra_%E2%80%93_Gradistea_Muntelui,_Muntii_Sureanu,_Hunedoara,_Romania_19.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sarmizegetusa Regia was the capital and the most important military, religious and political centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacians">Dacians</a> before their wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. It stood on top of a mountain 1,200 metres high, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orastie-mountains">Orăștie Mountains</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, and its remains lie in the village of Grădiștea de Munte in Hunedoara County. It should not be confused with Ulpia Traiana Sarmizegetusa, the Roman capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a> built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> about 40 kilometres away.</p><p>The fortress was the core of a defensive system of six Dacian fortresses in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orastie-mountains">Orăștie Mountains</a>: Sarmizegetusa, Costești-Blidaru, Piatra Roșie, Costești-Cetățuie, Căpâlna and Bănița. All six are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>. The citadel was built on five terraces, on an area of almost 30,000 square metres, of massive stone blocks in the style called murus dacicus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Towards the end of his reign, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burebista">Burebista</a> moved the Geto-Dacian capital from Argedava to Sarmizegetusa. It served as the Dacian capital for at least a century and a half and reached its peak under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decebalus">Decebalus</a>, whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> defeated in the reign of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>. Finds from the site include a medical kit in a brass-bound wooden box, containing a scalpel, tweezers, powdered pumice and miniature pots for medicines, a huge vase with an inscription in the Roman alphabet, DECEBAL PER SCORILO, meaning Decebalus, son of Scorilus.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> dismantled the walls in part at the end of the First Dacian War in 102 and rebuilt them as Roman fortifications. These were later destroyed, possibly by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacians">Dacians</a>, and rebuilt again after the successful Roman siege of 105-6. The Romans then built a new capital, Colonia Ulpia Traiana Augusta Dacica Sarmizegetusa, 40 kilometres away. Emperor Hadrian wanted it seen as the successor to the Dacian one, and so added the name Sarmizegetusa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The sacred zone lies about 100 metres east of the citadel gate, on a terrace joined to it by a paved road. It includes some of the largest circular and rectangular Dacian sanctuaries. The bases of the columns of the rectangular temples are still visible in regular rows. The large circular sanctuary was a setting of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> posts shaped like a D inside a timber circle and a low stone kerb, and its layout resembles <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stonehenge">Stonehenge</a>. In 2011 workers building a car park for visitors, paid for by the Hunedoara County Council and done without archaeologists' approval, destroyed part of the site and endangered about 30 metres of the citadel wall.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sarmizegetusa Regia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The heart of the kingdom lay in the Orăștie Mountains, where fortresses and sanctuaries ringed the capital, <b>Sarmizegetusa Regia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-dacia">Kingdom of Dacia</a></li></ul></section>
